'''The von Neumann bicommutant theorem''' is a fundamental result in the theory of operator algebras, which states that for any Von Neumann algebra ''M'' (a *-subalgebra of the bounded operators on a Hilbert space that is closed in the weak operator topology), the bicommutant of ''M'' is equal to ''M''. In other words, we have:

:$M = M''$,

where the commutant of ''M'', denoted $M'$, is the set of all bounded operators on the Hilbert space that commute with every element of ''M'', and the bicommutant of ''M'', denoted $M''$, is the commutant of $M'$.

This theorem has important consequences for the structure of Von Neumann algebras, as it shows that the weak closure of any *-subalgebra of the bounded operators on a Hilbert space is a Von Neumann algebra. Moreover, it plays a crucial role in the proof of the double commutant theorem for C*-algebras, which states that the normal elements of a C*-algebra form a Von Neumann algebra.

The von Neumann bicommutant theorem was first proved by John von Neumann in the 1930s, as part of his groundbreaking work on the foundations of quantum mechanics. It has since become a cornerstone of the theory of operator algebras and has been generalized and extended in various directions, including to the context of operator algebras acting on Banach spaces and to the setting of von Neumann algebras with additional structure.

