{{About|important theorem in operator algebras|Von Neumann bicommutant theorem}}

'''The Von Neumann Bicommutant Theorem''' is a crucial concept in the study of operator algebras, which tells us that any Von Neumann algebra is equal to its bicommutant. To be more specific, if we have a Von Neumann algebra $M$ (which is a *-subalgebra of the bounded operators on a Hilbert space that is closed in the weak operator topology), we will find that its bicommutant, denoted as $M''$, is the same as $M$:

$M = M''$

Here, the commutant of $M$, denoted as $M'$, is the set of all bounded operators on the Hilbert space that commute with every element of $M$. The bicommutant of $M$, $M''$, is then the commutant of the commutant, $M'$.

This theorem has significant implications for comprehending the structure of Von Neumann algebras. It shows us that the weak closure of any *-subalgebra of the bounded operators on a Hilbert space is a Von Neumann algebra. Furthermore, this theorem plays an essential role in proving the double commutant theorem for C*-algebras, which is used to demonstrate that the normal elements of a C*-algebra form a Von Neumann algebra.

The Von Neumann Bicommutant Theorem was first proven by John von Neumann in the 1930s as part of his innovative work on quantum mechanics' foundations. Since then, it has become a vital part of operator algebra theory and has been broadened and developed in various directions, such as operator algebras acting on Banach spaces and Von Neumann algebras with additional structures.

