In biochemistry, a '''cyclic nucleotide''' is a nucleotide in which the sugar component (a ribose or deoxyribose) forms a ring with one of the phosphate groups. The two main members of this group are '''cyclic adenosine monophosphate''' (cAMP) and '''cyclic guanosine monophosphate''' (cGMP), both of which play important roles as second messengers in many biological processes.

Cyclic nucleotides are synthesized from the corresponding 5'-nucleotide by '''adenylyl cyclase''' or '''guanylyl cyclase''', which catalyzes the formation of the cyclic phosphate group by attack of the 3'-hydroxyl group of the ribose. They are broken down to their linear counterparts by '''phosphodiesterase'''. Since the single phosphate group in the cyclic nucleotide is esterified to two alcohol groups, it is not acidic and does not form a phosphate anion at neutral pH.

Cyclic nucleotides act as second messengers by binding to and activating specific '''cyclic nucleotide-binding domains''' (CNBDs) on target proteins. These domains are often part of protein kinases and '''cyclic nucleotide-gated ion channels'''. The activation of these proteins can have diverse effects, such as modulation of ion transport, enzyme activity, and gene expression.

Cyclic nucleotides have been implicated in a wide range of physiological processes, including metabolism, learning and memory, cardiovascular function, and immune response. Dysregulation of cyclic nucleotide signaling has been associated with various diseases, including cancer, cardiovascular disease, and neurodegenerative disorders.

